Errata applied automatically
============================

A LX2160A / LX2162A board can include one file and it gets every work-around
its silicon revision needs:

    lx2160a_auto_errata.rcwi        rev 1
    lx2160a_rev2_auto_errata.rcwi   rev 2

It replaces the #include of lx2160a.rcwi so the board is not listing errata
one #include at a time any more.

Why
---

A board that names its errata by hand is only as complete as the day it
was written. Boards live downstream for years: a work-around added
upstream afterwards never reaches them and nothing says so.

Using it
--------

Declare the SerDes protocols as macros, then include the catalog. This
is lx2160ardb_rev2/IGGFF_PP_HHHH_RR_18_5_2 in full:

    /*
     * SerDes Protocol 1 -  18
     * SerDes Protocol 2 -  5
     * SerDes Protocol 3 -  2
     */

    #define SRDS_PRTCL_S1_VAL 18
    #define SRDS_PRTCL_S2_VAL 5
    #define SRDS_PRTCL_S3_VAL 2

    #include <../lx2160asi/lx2160a_rev2_auto_errata.rcwi>

    SYS_PLL_RAT=14
    ...
    SRDS_PLL_REF_CLK_SEL_S1=2
    ...

    /* the board's own PBI, unchanged */
    #include <../lx2160asi/scratchrw1.rcw>
    #include <../lx2160asi/bootlocptr_nor.rcw>
    #include <../lx2160asi/common.rcw>
    #include <../lx2160asi/flexspi_divisor_28.rcw>

The catalog assigns SRDS_PRTCL_S1, S2 and S3 from those macros, so a
board states each protocol once. It does not need to write the
assignments itself.

The include belongs at the top of the board file, where
lx2160a.rcwi used to be, and the errata are now the first PBI the
Service Processor executes rather than the last.

What a board gets
-----------------

Only the writes its protocols justify. On the example above, protocol 18
puts no PCIe lane on SerDes 1, so PEX1 and PEX2 fall out of a011270,
a050234, a050426 and a050479, and the board goes from 460 to 268 bytes
of PBI.

A board that declares no protocol is still correct: every erratum is
emitted at full size, exactly as before. The declaration is opt-in and
no existing board changes behaviour by accident.

serdes_prtcl.rcw holds the mapping.

Checking it
-----------

    ./check_errata_trim.sh [reference-rev]

builds the tree three times:
 - the reference revision,
 - the current tree with RCW_ERRATA_NO_TRIM defined,
 - the current tree normally
and asserts that the reference sits between the last two:
    trimmed  <=  reference  <=  no-trim
